/**
 * Encrypted-PDF fixtures for the pdf-lib-unreadable document class (oss-377).
 *
 * Real-world repro: a carrier PDF that combines the classic owner-password
 * encryption (Standard security handler, R=2, EMPTY user password — the
 * "no-print restriction" pattern insurance carriers and law firms ship
 * routinely) with a page tree stored in compressed object streams (ObjStm).
 * pdf-lib's `ignoreEncryption: true` SKIPS decryption rather than performing
 * it, so those object streams never inflate, the page tree is invisible, and
 * counting/slicing throw `Expected instance of PDFDict, but got instance of
 * undefined`. PDFium/MuPDF/pdfjs all decrypt the empty-user-password case
 * transparently and read the same file fine.
 *
 * Every page carries a real text object ("Fixture page N") so tests can catch
 * content-fidelity bugs, not just structural ones — a normalize that leaves
 * encryption live produces structurally valid slices with BLANK pages.
 *
 * Generated with pikepdf (Python):
 *
 *   pdf = pikepdf.new()
 *   font = pdf.make_indirect(pikepdf.Dictionary(
 *       Type=pikepdf.Name.Font, Subtype=pikepdf.Name.Type1,
 *       BaseFont=pikepdf.Name.Helvetica))
 *   for i in range(N):
 *       page = pdf.add_blank_page(page_size=(612, 792))
 *       page.Contents = pdf.make_stream(
 *           f"BT /F1 12 Tf 72 720 Td (Fixture page {i+1}) Tj ET".encode())
 *       page.Resources = pikepdf.Dictionary(Font=pikepdf.Dictionary(F1=font))
 *   pdf.save(path,
 *       encryption=pikepdf.Encryption(user="", owner="test-owner", R=2,
 *                                     metadata=False, aes=False),
 *       object_stream_mode=pikepdf.ObjectStreamMode.generate)
 *
 * `ENCRYPTED_OBJSTM_PDF_40_NORMALIZED` is the 40-page fixture re-saved through
 * pypdfium2 with `FPDF_REMOVE_SECURITY` — byte-for-byte what the parse
 * service's `/normalize-pdf` endpoint returns for it. pdf-lib loads and
 * slices it, and the slices keep their text.
 */

